## Short Answer

For **large, complex object graphs**, `System.Text.Json` in .NET 7 is **significantly faster** than `Newtonsoft.Json` — typically **2–4x** for serialization and **3–6x** for deserialization. This is not negligible. But the gap has narrowed considerably from early .NET Core days, and there are important caveats.

## Why the Gap Exists (and Has Narrowed)

### The Historical Context

```
.NET Core 2.1/3.1:   System.Text.Json was ~2-5x faster but had limited features
.NET 5/6:            Performance improved, feature parity grew
.NET 7:              Closest to feature parity with Newtonsoft; performance still leads
```

The performance advantage stems from fundamental architectural differences:

| Aspect | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) |
|---|---|---|
| Reflection | Heavy runtime reflection + caching | Compiled serializers (`JsonSerializerContext`) + optimized reflection |
| Memory Allocation | Higher allocation rate | Lower allocation via `Span<T>`, `ArrayPool`, struct-based parsing |
| Thread Safety | Reader/Writer pools | More efficient internal pooling |
| Source Generation | Not available | **Native AOT-compatible source-generated serializers** |

