## Where the Advantage Is Most Pronounced

1. **High-throughput APIs** (thousands of requests/sec): The allocation difference compounds significantly. STJ's span-based approach reduces GC pressure dramatically.

2. **Large collections**: Lists/dictionaries with hundreds/thousands of elements show the biggest gains because STJ avoids per-element boxing/unboxing and string intermediaries.

3. **Deep nesting**: Source generators unroll loops at compile time, whereas Newtonsoft recurses through reflection-heavy paths.

## Where the Difference Narrows or Reverses

| Factor | Impact |
|---|---|
| **Simple DTOs** (< 10 properties) | Difference shrinks to ~1.5–2× |
| **First-run / cold start** | Source gen has zero runtime cost; Newtonsoft has one-time reflection cache build (~5–20ms) |
| **Custom converters** | Newtonsoft's converter model is more mature and flexible; STJ's custom converters are slightly slower due to interface indirection |
| **Circular references** | Newtonsoft handles them gracefully by default; STJ requires explicit configuration (`ReferenceHandler.Preserve`) which adds overhead |
| **Legacy property naming conventions** | Newtonsoft's `[JsonProperty]` and camelCase settings are battle-tested; STJ's options work but edge cases exist |

---

## Migration Considerations Beyond Raw Speed

### ✅ Reasons to Migrate

- **GC pressure reduction**: Fewer short-lived objects mean less frequent Gen 0/1 collections, which improves tail latency.
- **Security**: `System.Text.Json` is actively maintained by Microsoft; Newtonsoft is community-maintained.
- **.NET 7 features**: Async streams, improved diagnostics, better memory pooling.
- **Long-term support**: .NET Framework 4.8 reaches end-of-life soon; .NET 7 LTS extends through May 2024 (consider .NET 8 LTS for production).

### ⚠️ Caveats

1. **Source generators require project configuration changes**:
   ```xml
   <!-- In your .csproj -->
   <ItemGroup>
     <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="7.0.0" />
   </ItemGroup>

   <!-- Enable source generation context -->
