## Verdict

**For large, complex object graphs in .NET 7:**

| Factor | Assessment |
|---|---|
| Raw speed | **System.Text.Json wins by 2–4×**, especially with source generators |
| Memory/GC | **System.Text.Json wins decisively** — lower allocations matter at scale |
| Feature parity | **Mostly there**; polymorphism and some edge-case converters need work |
| Migration effort | **Moderate** — mostly mechanical, but requires testing for behavioral diffs |
| Long-term viability | **.NET Framework 4.8 is end-of-life**; .NET 7+ is the only forward path |

The performance gain is **not negligible**. It is significant enough to justify the migration effort on its own, and when combined with the broader benefits of moving off .NET Framework (cross-platform, modern tooling, active security updates, cloud-native features), the decision strongly favors migrating.

If your workload is I/O-bound rather than CPU-bound, you may not see throughput improvements in end-to-end latency, but you will see reduced memory pressure and lower infrastructure costs — which often matters more in production at scale.

