# System.Text.Json vs Newtonsoft.Json: Performance Analysis for .NET 7 Migration

## Short Answer

**Yes, the performance gain is significant and absolutely holds up for large, complex object graphs.** `System.Text.Json` in .NET 7 consistently outperforms `Newtonsoft.Json` by **2–10x** depending on the scenario, with the gap widening as object complexity increases.

---

## Why the Gap Widens with Complexity

### Architectural Differences

| Aspect | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) |
|---|---|---|
| Serialization approach | Reflection-heavy, virtual method dispatch | Source-generated IL + optimized reflection fallback |
| Memory allocation | High (many intermediate objects) | Low (Span<T>, stackalloc, pooled buffers) |
| Parsing model | String-based intermediate steps | UTF-8 direct parsing (zero-copy where possible) |
| Custom converters | Virtual/dispatch overhead | Inlined via source generators |
| Threading model | Not inherently concurrent | Designed for high-concurrency workloads |

### Key Optimizations in .NET 7's `System.Text.Json`

```csharp
// 1. Source Generators eliminate reflection entirely at runtime
[JsonSerializable(typeof(MyComplexGraph))]
internal partial class MyContext : JsonSerializerContext { }

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    TypeInfoResolver = MyContext.Default
};
// At compile time, this generates IL equivalent to hand-written serialization code.
// No reflection, no virtual calls, full inlining potential.

// 2. UTF-8 native support avoids encoding/decoding overhead
byte[] utf8Data = await stream.ReadAllAsync();
var obj = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MyComplexGraph>(utf8Data, options);

// 3. Pooled memory reduces GC pressure under load
// Internal implementation uses ArrayPool<byte> and Stackalloc<T>
```

