# System.Text.Json vs Newtonsoft.Json: Performance in .NET 7 Migration

## Short Answer

**Yes, the performance gain is significant and absolutely holds up for large, complex object graphs.** `System.Text.Json` in .NET 7 is consistently **2–5x faster** than Newtonsoft.Json for most serialization/deserialization scenarios, with even larger gaps on large payloads. The improvement is not negligible—it's one of the strongest arguments for migrating.

## Why the Gap Exists

### Architectural Differences

| Aspect | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) |
|---|---|---|
| Memory allocation | Heavy reflection + many intermediate objects | Source-generated code paths + minimal allocations |
| Reflection usage | Extensive runtime reflection | Optional source generators (zero-reflection path) |
| String handling | Creates many string intermediaries | Uses `ReadOnlySpan<char>` and pooled buffers |
| Thread safety | Per-thread caching | Designed for concurrent use with pooling |
| Custom converters | Required for optimization | Built-in high-performance defaults |

### Key .NET 7 Enhancements to System.Text.Json

```csharp
