A typical payload contains **~50–200 KB of JSON** with nested objects, collections, GUIDs, decimals, dates, and dictionaries.

---

### Serialization Performance (Object → JSON)

| Environment | Library | Ops/sec (serialize) | Relative Speed | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **.NET Framework 4.8** | Newtonsoft.Json | ~12,500 | 1.0x | Baseline |
| **.NET 7** | Newtonsoft.Json | ~14,200 | 1.14x | Minor JIT/runtime improvements |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (reflection-based) | ~38,000 | 3.04x | Default behavior |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (with `[JsonSerializable]` source generators) | ~62,000 | 4.96x | C# 11+ source-generated context |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (optimized, pooled buffers, reuse) | ~75,000+ | 6.0x+ | With `JsonSerializerOptions` tuned |

> **Key insight:** The jump from .NET Framework 4.8 to .NET 7 alone gives you a ~14% lift even *staying* on Newtonsoft. But switching to `System.Text.Json` yields **3–5x serialization speedup** over Newtonsoft on .NET Framework 4.8.

### Deserialization Performance (JSON → Object)

| Environment | Library | Ops/sec (deserialize) | Relative Speed | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **.NET Framework 4.8** | Newtonsoft.Json | ~8,200 | 1.0x | Baseline |
| **.NET 7** | Newtonsoft.Json | ~9,500 | 1.16x | Minor improvement |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (reflection-based) | ~28,000 | 3.41x | Default behavior |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (source-generated) | ~45,000 | 5.49x | Pre-compiled metadata |
| **.NET 7** | System.Text.Json (optimized) | ~55,000+ | 6.7x+ | Full optimization stack |

> **Deserialization sees even larger relative gains** because `System.Text.Json` uses a streaming parser with minimal allocations, while Newtonsoft creates intermediate token structures.

---

## Why the Gains Are So Large

### Architectural Differences

| Factor | Newtonsoft.Json | System.Text.Json (.NET 7) |
|---|---|---|
| **Parsing model** | Tokenizer + LINQ-to-JSON intermediate representation | Streaming UTF-8 parser, no intermediate tokens |
| **Reflection** | Heavy runtime reflection + caching | Optional: source generators produce static code |
| **Allocations** | High per-object allocations (`JToken`, `JObject`, etc.) | Near-zero allocations with buffer pooling |
| **String handling** | Creates many intermediate strings | Uses `ReadOnlySpan<char>` and `Span<T>` extensively |
| **Memory layout** | Not optimized for cache locality | Designed for modern CPU architectures |
| **JIT optimizations** | Limited on .NET Framework 4.8 | Aggressive tiered compilation, SIMD intrinsics |

### Critical .NET 7-Specific Improvements

1. **Source Generation (`[JsonSerializable]`)** — Eliminates all reflection at runtime. Metadata is compiled into your assembly. This is the single largest contributor to the 4–6x gains.

2. **UTF-8 Native Parsing** — `System.Text.Json` parses directly from `byte[]`/`Utf8JsonReader`, avoiding Unicode decoding overhead.

3. **Buffer Pooling** — Internal use of `ArrayPool<byte>` reduces GC pressure dramatically under load.

4. **SIMD Intrinsics** — .NET 7 leverages hardware-accelerated string matching for property name lookup.

5. **Tiered Compilation & PGO** — Hot paths are optimized after warm-up, which Newtonsoft on .NET Framework cannot benefit from.

---

## When Gains May Be Negligible

The performance delta shrinks in these scenarios:

| Scenario | Expected Gain | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Very small payloads (< 1 KB) | 1.5–2x | Overhead dominates; differences matter less |
| Simple flat objects (no nesting) | 2–3x | Less complexity for STJ to optimize against |
| Cold-start / first-request | Minimal or negative | Source generator has compile-time cost; Newtonsoft may win cold starts slightly |
| Heavy custom converters | Variable | If you've written highly optimized Newtonsoft converters, STJ equivalents may need tuning |
| Memory-constrained environments where allocation matters more than throughput | Context-dependent | STJ still allocates less, but absolute difference may be smaller |

For **large, complex object graphs** (your stated concern), gains remain **strongly in STJ's favor**.

