## Reproducible Benchmark Code

You can verify these numbers yourself:

```csharp
using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;
using BenchmarkDotNet.Running;
using System.Text.Json;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

[BenchmarkRunner]
public class JsonBenchmarks
{
    private readonly Person _person;
    private readonly string _json;
    private readonly byte[] _utf8Json;

    public JsonBenchmarks()
    {
        _person = GenerateComplexPerson(); // Your typical large object
        _json = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(_person);
        _utf8Json = JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(_person);
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public string NewtonsoftSerialize() =>
        JsonConvert.SerializeObject(_person);

    [Benchmark]
    public byte[] StjSerialize() =>
        JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(_person);

    [Benchmark]
    public Person NewtonsoftDeserialize() =>
        JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<Person>(_json);

    [Benchmark]
    public Person StjDeserialize() =>
        JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Person>(_utf8Json);
}
```

Run this on both **.NET Framework 4.8** and **.NET 7** to see the delta firsthand. Expect results within ±10% of the table above depending on hardware.

